{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Hmm, still not 134. The \"134 处\" for root may have counted differently — maybe including \"agent\" (bare) or maybe the research-report-E N3.1' count of 123 is closer to a real grep. Honestly the exact 243 reproduction is not needed for my test script.\n\nThe critical thing: my test script must define ONE regex, compute baseline (which the task will record before migration), and after migration the count must be strictly lower.\n\nBut wait — there's a critical subtlety about how the baseline is captured. The test method says:\n\n\"操作 = 迁移前先以验收同一正则捕获自然语言触发词基线计数并落盘（如 .rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt）→ 迁移 → ...\"\n\nSo the DOING task (task11 implementation) is supposed to:\n1. Capture the baseline count using the acceptance regex and write it to `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt`.\n2. Perform the migration.\n\nThe test script (which I'm writing now) should:\n1. Read the baseline from `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt`.\n2. Compute the current count using the SAME regex.\n3. Assert current < baseline.\n\nBut there's a chicken-and-egg: the regex must be shared between the baseline capture (done by the implementer) and the test script (done by me). Since I'm writing the test script FIRST (TDD RED phase), and the implementer will later do the migration and capture the baseline, the implementer needs to know which regex to use. The test script should DOCUMENT the regex clearly in its code/comments so the implementer uses the same one. Alternatively, the test script itself could capture the baseline if the baseline file doesn't exist.\n\nHmm, but \"只生成测试脚本，不要执行任务本身\" — I should not perform the task. But the baseline capture is PART of the task (the \"操作\" includes \"迁移前先以验收同一正则捕获自然语言触发词基线计数并落盘\"). So the implementer does the baseline capture. My test script should just READ the baseline file.\n\nHowever, for the test script to be robust and idempotent, and to give a meaningful RED result now, I need to decide: if the baseline file doesn't exist yet, what should the test do?\n\nOptions:\nA. Fail with an error \"trigger-baseline.txt does not exist\" — this is a valid RED failure (the task hasn't created the baseline file yet). This aligns with TDD: the test should fail because the feature (migration + baseline) hasn't been done.\n\nB. If baseline missing, compute the baseline now and write it (self-healing). But that would make the test \"do the task\" partially, and could mask issues.\n\nGiven TDD and the test method, option A is cleaner: the baseline file is a PRECONDITION. If it doesn't exist, the test fails with a clear message.\n\nActually, let me re-read the test method normal path: \"前置条件 = task5/9 完成\". The precondition is task5/9 completion, not the baseline file. The operation includes creating the baseline file. So the baseline file creation is part of the OPERATION (the task), not a precondition.\n\nBut for the test to verify \"迁移后自然语言触发词计数 < 基线\", it needs the baseline. So the test must check the baseline file exists, and if not, report it as an error (the migration task hasn't completed its first step).\n\nSo my test script's normal path test:\n1. Check `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt` exists; if not → error.\n2. If exists, parse the baseline count (integer).\n3. Compute current natural-language trigger count using the same regex.\n4. Check pi syntax count ≥ 1.\n5. Assert current trigger count < baseline.\n\nBoundary test:\n6. Check real agent names count ≥ 1 file.\n\nException test:\n7. Check sense_loop.md contains '批判门禁|反向回流|judgment.md' (≥1).\n8. go build succeeds.\n9. `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -c '{{'` == 0.\n10. `go test ./internal/prompt/... -v` passes.\n\nNow, for the natural-language trigger regex, I need to finalize it. Let me use the authoritative phrase list from N1 F1. But I want a regex that:\n- Produces a meaningful baseline (nonzero now).\n- Will decrease after migration.\n\nGiven the migration replaces trigger imperatives, the safest is to use the SPECIFIC trigger phrases that will be removed, while avoiding generic \"subagent\" that will be re-introduced by the migration's own explanatory text (\"subagent 工具\").\n\nActually, let me reconsider. What does \"自然语言触发词\" mean precisely? It's the natural-language phrases that trigger/instruct subagent dispatch. From N1 F1: 「派发 subagent」「每个 subagent 独立启动」「SPAWN Sub Agent」「Main Agent / Sub Agent」「父 Agent / 子 Agent」.\n\nThe migration will rewrite these. But will it eliminate \"父 Agent\" and \"子 Agent\" and \"Main Agent\"/\"Sub Agent\" entirely? Possibly not — the task's own KR3.3 mentions \"parent\" and \"普通子 agent 不持 subagent 工具\", and \"编排权在 parent\". It might keep \"父 Agent\"（parent orchestrator）and \"子 Agent\"（child agent）as role descriptions while adding explicit `agent:'worker'` etc.\n\nThe acceptance criterion is just \"迁移后自然语言触发词计数 < 基线\" (strictly less). Even if some terms remain, as long as the total decreases, it passes.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, I think the most robust choice is to use a regex that captures the FULL set of natural-language trigger phrases listed in the RFC (the 5 phrases + common variants), which is exactly what \"自然语言触发词\" refers to. These phrases WILL be largely eliminated by migration (e.g., \"SPAWN Sub Agent\" → runs.run, \"派发 subagent\" → runs.run). Let me use:\n\n```\n派发 subagent|每个 subagent 独立启动|SPAWN Sub Agent|Main Agent|Sub Agent|父 Agent|子 Agent\n```\n\nWait, but \"Main Agent\"/\"Sub Agent\"/\"父 Agent\"/\"子 Agent\" might partially remain as role labels. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what the implementer will do, and what will DEFINITELY decrease.\n\nThe most unambiguous \"trigger words\" that will be replaced are:\n- 「派发 subagent」 (dispatch subagent)\n- 「SPAWN Sub Agent」 (spawn sub agent)\n- 「SPAWN Step N 子 Agent」 (spawn step N sub agent)\n\nThese imperative phrases WILL be replaced by `runs.run(...)`. Let me verify these are the words that currently appear and will definitely go away.\n\nBut the acceptance also says \"自然语言触发词显著下降\" which is broader.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's the RIGHT thing to do here as a test author. I should define the regex to match the natural language trigger terms that the task's migration is contractually obligated to remove/replace, and make it deterministic and documented. The task goal explicitly lists: 「派发 subagent」「SPAWN Sub Agent」「子 Agent」等.\n\nI'll define the regex as follows (case-insensitive for English terms, since \"Sub Agent\" appears with various casing):\n\n```\n派发 subagent|SPAWN Sub Agent|SPAWN Step|子 Agent|父 Agent|Main Agent|Sub Agent|subagent\n```\n\nWait, but \"subagent\" is too risky (migration adds \"subagent 工具\").\n\nLet me be more careful and exclude bare \"subagent\". Instead use the phrase-level terms:\n\nPattern (Python regex, case-insensitive):\n```\n派发\\s*subagent|SPAWN\\s+Sub\\s+Agent|SPAWN\\s+Step|子\\s*Agent|父\\s*Agent|Main\\s+Agent|Sub\\s+Agent|每个\\s+subagent\\s+独立启动\n```\n\nThis matches the trigger phrases but not bare \"subagent\" or bare \"agent\".\n\nLet me count occurrences with this pattern."}}
