he core trigger terms are:\n- \"派发 subagent\" (dispatch subagent) — imperative trigger\n- \"SPAWN Sub Agent\" — imperative trigger\n- \"子 Agent\" / \"父 Agent\" / \"Main Agent\" / \"Sub Agent\" — role names that get mapped to real agent names\n- \"每个 subagent 独立启动\" — description of spawning\n\nI think a good, defensible regex is the union of these specific phrases. This is literally what N1 F1 lists as the natural-language terms. Let me go with the phrase-level regex (without bare \"subagent\"/\"派发\").\n\nBut I realize there's still the \"Sub Agent\" vs \"SubAgent\" and \"子 Agent\" with/without space issues. Let me handle variants. Also note \"main agent\" appears lowercase in sense_loop.md and research.md (2+1 occurrences). My case-insensitive match handles that.\n\nLet me finalize the regex. I'll define it in Python with re.IGNORECASE and search all files under templates/:\n\n```python\nTRIGGER_PATTERN = re.compile(\n    r'派发\\s*subagent'\n    r'|SPAWN\\s+Sub\\s+Agent'\n    r'|SPAWN\\s+Step'\n    r'|子\\s*Agent'\n    r'|父\\s*Agent'\n    r'|Main\\s+Agent'\n    r'|Sub\\s*Agent'\n    r'|每个\\s+subagent\\s+独立启动',\n    re.IGNORECASE\n)\n```\n\nCount = total number of matches across all files (using finditer, counting non-overlapping matches).\n\nCurrent count with this regex = 109 (from my grep). But wait, \"Sub\\s*Agent\" would also match \"SubAgent\" and \"Sub Agent\". Let me make sure \"Main\\s+Agent\" doesn't match \"main agent\" — with IGNORECASE it does.\n\nActually, let me simplify and make it robust. But I want to avoid double-counting overlaps. Python's finditer counts non-overlapping. E.g., \"Main Agent / Sub Agent\" → \"Main Agent\" matches, then \"/ Sub Agent\" → \"Sub Agent\" matches. Good.\n\nLet me verify the Python count matches my grep. I'll just trust finditer.\n\nNow, an important concern: after migration, will this count DECREASE? The migration replaces:\n- \"SPAWN Sub Agent\" (3 occurrences) → runs.run ✓ removed\n- \"SPAWN Step\" (7) → runs.run ✓ removed\n- \"派发 subagent\" (4) → runs.run ✓ removed\n- \"每个 subagent 独立启动\" (1) → runs.run ✓ removed\n- \"Main Agent\" (21) → may become \"parent\"/\"main agent orchestrator\" — will it remain? The doing_loop/gen-loop \"Main Agent / Sub Agent\" structure will likely be rewritten. KR3.3 says \"doing/easy（Main/Sub Agent）\" needs migration. So \"Main Agent\"/\"Sub Agent\" role labels will likely be replaced by explicit agent references or \"parent orchestrator\" + \"child agent (agent:'worker')\".\n- \"子 Agent\"/\"父 Agent\" (44) → learning_loop has many; KR3.3 mentions \"learning（父/子 Agent）\" needs migration. So these will be rewritten.\n\nSo the count should significantly decrease. Good.\n\nBut there's still risk: the migration might ADD text like \"父 Agent\" in explanations (e.g., \"编排权在 parent（父 Agent）\"). But the acceptance is just \"count < baseline\", and \"显著下降\" is the goal. Even if a few remain, it'll be less than 109. I'll go with this.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to make the test more robust by NOT over-fitting. The acceptance criterion in the test method is: \"迁移后自然语言触发词计数 < 基线\". The baseline is what the implementer captures. As long as the regex is the same, the comparison is internally consistent.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: the implementer will capture the baseline using MY regex (from the test script). If the implementer can't easily reproduce my Python regex in grep, they might capture a different number. To mitigate, I should document the regex clearly in the test script AND make the test script itself able to COMPUTE the current count and, if baseline file is missing, the test script could WRITE the baseline (but that would \"do the task\").\n\nActually, re-reading the test method more carefully:\n\n\"操作 = **迁移前先以验收同一正则捕获自然语言触发词基线计数并落盘**（如 `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt`）→ 迁移 → ...\"\n\nThis is the TASK's operation, not the test's. The test verifies. So the implementer captures baseline. The test reads it.\n\nI think the cleanest design: the test script defines the regex and a `count_trigger_words()` function. The test reads the baseline file. If the baseline file is missing, that's an error (RED). If present, compare.\n\nTo make the implementer's life easy and ensure consistency, I'll ALSO make the test script capable of writing the baseline when run with a special flag `--write-baseline` (or when the file doesn't exist, offer to compute and store it). Hmm, but that complicates and the prompt says don't do the task.\n\nActually, simplest and cleanest: \n- The test script defines the regex and count function.\n- It reads the baseline file (plain integer).\n- It compares.\n\nThe implementer, when doing the migration, will read this test script (it's part of the repo), see the regex, capture the baseline with the same regex, write it to the file, then migrate. That's the workflow. I'll add a clear comment in the script explaining the regex and that the implementer must capture the baseline using `python3 task11.py --dump-baseline` or a documented grep command.\n\nActually, I could make the script self-sufficient: if run with `--dump-baseline`, it writes the current count to the baseline file and exits. This gives the implementer a deterministic way to capture the baseline with EXACTLY the same regex. This is not \"doing the task\" (the migration); it's providing tooling. But the prompt says \"只生成测试脚本，不要执行任务本身\" — generating a test script with a baseline-dump helper is fine, as long as I don't RUN the dump now.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a risk: if I provide `--dump-baseline`, and the test auto-dumps when file is missing, the test would never be RED for the baseline step (it would self-heal). That violates TDD. So I'll NOT auto-dump. Instead:\n\n- Provide `--dump-baseline` as an explicit, separate mode (opt-in), so the implementer can capture the baseline deterministically. The main test mode (no args) requires the file to exist and fails if missing.\n\nWait, but actually, thinking about it more: the test framework will run `python3 task11.py` with no args. In that mode, if the baseline file doesn't exist, it should fail with \"trigger-baseline.txt does not exist — run migration's baseline capture step first\". That's the correct RED behavior.\n\nLet me also consider: should the test write the baseline if missing? NO. Keep it pure.\n\nNow let me also think about the `--dump-baseline` helper. Actually, I worry this adds complexity and potential for the grader to misinterpret. But it's genuinely useful for consistency. Let me include it but make it clearly opt-in and NOT called in normal test mode.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole scope. The test script must:\n1. Define regex + count function.\n2. Normal path test.\n3. Boundary test.\n4. Exception test.\n5. Output JSON to stdout, exit code.\n\nLet me also verify: the `go build` and `go test` and `./bin/rick plan --dry-run` commands. I need to run them from the repo root (cwd). The test script will be at `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests/task11.py`. The repo root is 4 levels up: tests → doing → job_35 → .rick → root? Let me compute.\n\nPath: `/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests/task11.py`\n\n- dirname = .../doing/tests\n- dirname(dirname) = .../doing\n- dirname x3 = .../job_35\n- dirname x4 = .../.rick\n- dirname x5 = .../rick (repo root)\n\nSo `project_root = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))))))` = 5 levels up.\n\nWait let me count carefully:\n- __file__ = /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests/task11.py\n- dirname #1 = /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests\n- dirname #2 = /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick/jobs/job_35/doing\n- dirname #3 = /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick/jobs/job_35\n- dirname #4 = /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick\n- dirname #5 = /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick ← repo root\n\nSo 5 dirname calls. Go