ATTERN = re.compile(\n    r'派发\\s*subagent'\n    r'|SPAWN\\s+Sub\\s+Agent'\n    r'|SPAWN\\s+Step'\n    r'|子\\s*Agent'\n    r'|父\\s*Agent'\n    r'|Main\\s+Agent'\n    r'|Sub\\s*Agent'\n    r'|每个\\s+subagent\\s+独立启动',\n    re.IGNORECASE,\n)\n\nPI_SYNTAX_PATTERN = re.compile(r'workflowScript|runs\\.run|runs\\.all')\nAGENT_NAME_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"agent:'worker'|agent:'reviewer'|agent:'think'|agent:'research'|agent:'exporter'\")\nSENSE_PATTERN = re.compile(r'批判门禁|反向回流|judgment\\.md')\n```\n\nWait, I need to reconsider `runs\\.run` — the dot in \"runs.run\" needs escaping in a regex, otherwise `.` matches any char. I'll escape it. Also `runs.all`.\n\nFor counting files that contain pi syntax: the test method uses:\n`grep -rcE 'workflowScript|runs\\.run|runs\\.all' internal/prompt/templates/ | grep -v ':0' | wc -l`\n\nSo it counts FILES with ≥1 match, not total occurrences. I'll replicate: count files under templates/ (recursively) that contain at least one match of PI_SYNTAX_PATTERN. Expected ≥1.\n\nFor real agent names: `grep -rcE \"agent:'worker'|...\" ... | grep -v ':0' | wc -l` → count files with ≥1 match. Expected ≥1.\n\nFor trigger word count: this is a COUNT (baseline vs current), so I'll count total occurrences (finditer) across all files, OR count files? The test method says \"自然语言触发词计数 < 基线\" — \"计数\" = count. The baseline is a count. I'll count total matches (occurrences). But to be consistent with how the baseline was captured, I must document that the baseline = total occurrences of TRIGGER_PATTERN across all files under templates/.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, there's ambiguity between \"lines with match\" vs \"occurrences\". Let me think. The RFC's \"243 处\" (处 = places/occurrences). The most natural interpretation of \"计数\" is total occurrences. I'll count total non-overlapping matches via finditer across all files recursively.\n\nBut let me reconsider — should the trigger count be total occurrences or number of matching LINES or number of files? The acceptance \"迁移后自然语言触发词计数 < 基线\" — a simple total occurrence count is cleanest and most sensitive to reduction. I'll use total occurrences.\n\nNow, one more concern: after migration, the migrated templates will contain `workflowScript` and `runs.run(...)` with task strings that might contain the word \"subagent\" or \"agent\". But my TRIGGER_PATTERN is phrase-based and won't match `runs.run('main', {agent:'worker', task:'...'})`. It won't match \"agent:'worker'\" (no \"Agent\" with capital A in pattern? Actually \"Main\\s+Agent\" requires \"Main Agent\". \"agent:'worker'\" is lowercase, won't match). Good.\n\nBut wait — could the migration introduce \"Sub Agent\" or \"Main Agent\" phrases in NEW explanatory text? Possibly, e.g., \"parent（Main Agent）编排\". But the net effect should still be a decrease because it removes many more than it adds. The acceptance is \"< baseline\", so partial remaining is fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: what if the migration keeps \"父 Agent\" as a role label but adds \"agent:'worker'\" alongside? E.g., learning_loop might become: \"每个 Step 由 parent（父 Agent）用 runs.run('step1', {agent:'worker', task:'...'}) 启动\". In that case \"父 Agent\" remains! Then my \"父 Agent\" (17 occurrences) might not decrease much.\n\nHmm. This is the crux. Which terms will DEFINITELY decrease?\n\nThe most reliable decrease: \"SPAWN Sub Agent\" and \"SPAWN Step\" and \"派发 subagent\" and \"每个 subagent 独立启动\" — these are imperative trigger phrases that MUST be replaced by explicit syntax (that's the whole point of the migration). They will go to ~0.\n\n\"Main Agent\"/\"Sub Agent\"/\"父 Agent\"/\"子 Agent\" — these are role NAMES. The migration's KR3.3 says: \"doing/easy（Main/Sub Agent）\" and \"learning（父/子 Agent）\" need migration. The migration maps these role names to real agent names (`agent:'worker'` etc.). So these role labels SHOULD be replaced/removed too. But some descriptive text might retain them.\n\nGiven the acceptance is just \"count < baseline\" (strictly less), and the goal is \"显著下降\" (significant decrease), even if only the imperative phrases decrease (3+7+4+1 = 15 occurrences) and some role names remain, the count WILL be < baseline as long as at least one decreases and none of the remaining increases.\n\nBut could role names INCREASE? The migration adds explicit `agent:'worker'` etc. It might also add \"parent orchestrator\" explanations. The risk of INCREASE is if the migration rewrites text in a way that adds MORE \"父 Agent\"/\"子 Agent\"/\"Main Agent\"/\"Sub Agent\" labels. Unlikely but possible.\n\nTo be safe, I should choose a regex that captures ONLY the terms that are UNAMBIGUOUSLY \"trigger words\" that the migration replaces — i.e., the imperative dispatch phrases, NOT the role labels. But the task goal explicitly lists 「子 Agent」 as a trigger word to migrate. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me re-read the task goal: \"把各命令模板中自然语言 subagent 触发词（「派发 subagent」「SPAWN Sub Agent」「子 Agent」等，共 243 处...）改写为显式 pi 触发语法\". So 「子 Agent」 IS a trigger word per the task. And the RFC F1 lists 「父 Agent / 子 Agent」 and 「Main Agent / Sub Agent」 as natural-language terms.\n\nGiven the acceptance just needs \"count < baseline\", and my regex captures 109 occurrences currently (with role names being the bulk: 子 Agent 22 + 父 Agent 17 + Sub Agent 13+10 + Main Agent 10+8 = 80 role names + 15 imperative phrases + 2 import_ctx 子 Agent + 2 main agent + ...), even if role names partially remain, the imperative phrases removal guarantees some decrease. And realistically the migration WILL replace role names too (that's the KR).\n\nActually, I realize I should think about what gives the most ROBUST and DEFENSIBLE test. Let me reconsider using the EXACT phrases from N1 F1 as the regex, matching them precisely. The five phrases:\n1. 派发 subagent\n2. 每个 subagent 独立启动\n3. SPAWN Sub Agent\n4. Main Agent / Sub Agent  (→ but this as a phrase includes \" / \" which may not appear)\n5. 父 Agent / 子 Agent\n\nBut exact phrase matching gives only 7 matches (I tested earlier). That's too sparse and would make the \"significantly decrease\" assertion weak (it's already tiny). Actually a tiny baseline is fine for the assertion \"current < baseline\", but \"显著下降\" is better demonstrated with a larger baseline.\n\nLet me take yet another step back. I think the cleanest, most defensible approach is:\n\nUse a regex matching the FULL natural-language trigger vocabulary, but EXCLUDE the generic English word \"subagent\" and \"agent\" (to avoid the migration's own added \"subagent 工具\"/\"agent:'worker'\" text inflating the count). Specifically match:\n\n- Chinese trigger/role terms: 派发, 子 Agent, 父 Agent (with flexible spacing)\n- English trigger/role terms: SPAWN (Sub Agent / Step), Main Agent, Sub Agent, SubAgent\n- \"每个 subagent 独立启动\" is covered by \"subagent\"? No, if I exclude bare subagent, I need it. Actually \"每个 subagent 独立启动\" contains \"subagent\" but I want to match the phrase. Let me include it explicitly.\n\nWait, but \"派发\" alone (bare) appears 23+5+... times and is a strong trigger verb. Should I include bare \"派发\"? The migration replaces \"派发 subagent\"/\"派发下一阶段\" etc. Actually \"派发下一阶段 OR 反向回流\" is SENSE-specific flow language, NOT subagent dispatch — it might be KEPT (it's about phase progression, not subagent). Similarly \"派发 research\"/\"派发 exporter\" ARE subagent dispatches.\n\nThis is getting too deep. Let me simplify and be pragmatic.\n\nDECISION: I'll define the regex as the union of the specific trigger terms listed in the RFC/task, at phrase level (case-insensitive), EXCLUDING bare \"subagent\"/\"agent\"/\"派发\". This is:\n\n```\n派发\\s*subagent|SPAWN\\s+Sub\\s+Agent|SPAWN\\s+Step|子\\s*Agent|父\\s*Agent|Main\\s+Agent|Sub\\s*Agent|每个\\s+subagent\\s+独立启动\n```\n\nThis yields 109 current occurrences. It's deterministic, documented, and the migration will reduce it (imperative phrases definitely, role names mostly).\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to include \"Sub\\s*Agent\" as a separate alternative when \"SPAWN\\s+Sub\\s+Agent\" already covers the \"SPAWN Sub Agent\" case. Yes, \"Sub\\s*Agent\" catches standalone \"Sub Agent\" (doing_loop/gen-loop \"Main Agent / Sub Agent\" pairs). Good.\n\nBut note: \"SPAWN\\s+Sub\\s+Agent\" vs \"Sub\\s*Agent\" — in Python regex with finditer (non-overlapping left-to-right), the alternation order matters. If \"SPAWN Sub Agent\" text appears, \"SPAWN\\s+Sub\\s+Agent\" (first alternative) matches the whole thing. Good, no double count.\n\nAlso \"Main\\s+Agent\" will match \"Main Agent\" (10+8). And \"main agent\" lowercase (2+1) via IGNORECASE. Good.\n\nNow, will \"Sub Agent\" (standalone, 13+10) decrease after migration? The doing_loop/gen-loop \"Main Agent / Sub Agent\" structure will be rewritten to \"parent orchestrator + runs.run(agent:'worker')\". Likely yes.\n\nOK. I'm confident enough. Let me also add a safety: the count comparison should be \"current < baseline\", and I should also report both numbers in the error message for debuggability.\n\nNow, another design point: the baseline file format. It should contain a single integer (the count). I'll parse it as: read all text, extract the first integer via regex `\\d+`, or try int(strip()). To be safe, I'll extract all digits and take the first integer. Actually simpler: `int(re.search(r'\\d+', content).group())`. But if the implementer writes a plain number like \"109\", int(content.strip()) works. I'll do: strip, then try int; if fails, regex extract first number; if still fails, error.\n\nLet me also think: should the test script's normal-path test require the baseline file FIRST, and if missing, report error but STILL run the other tests (pi syntax, agent names, etc.)? Yes — collect all errors, don't exit early.\n\nFor the trigger-count comparison: if baseline file missing, I can't compare, so I add an error about the missing baseline. But I can still report the current count (to stderr for debugging). The pi syntax check and agent name check still run independently.\n\nNow the exception test:\n1. sense_loop.md semantic keywords ≥1. Use grep count. If <1 → error.\n2. go build: run `go build ./...` in project_root, capture output; if returncode != 0 → error with output.\n3. `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -c '{{'` == 0: run the command, count `{{` occurrences in stdout. If count != 0 → error. Also if the command fails → error.\n4. `go test ./internal/prompt/... -v`: run, check returncode == 0. If not → error.\n\nNote: the test method for exception says: \"操作 = grep ... (预期 ≥1) + go build + ./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -c '{{' (预期 0); 预期 = 语义命中 ≥1 且无 {{ 泄漏，go test ./internal/prompt/... -v 全绿。\"\n\nSo the exception test includes go test. I'll include all.\n\nNow about running `go build` and `go test` in the test script — these take time (go build ~seconds, go test maybe cached). The timeout should be reasonable. I'll set a generous timeout (e.g., 300s) for go test.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether the test script should run these heavy commands EVERY time. The prompt requires implementing all test steps. So yes.\n\nOne more consideration: the `./bin/rick plan --dry-run` command writes \"2026/08/17 ... warn: LoadLoopsContext...\" to stderr and the plan prompt to stdout. The `grep -c '{{'` operates on stdout. Good. I'll capture stdout and count `{{`.\n\nBut wait — `plan --dry-run` might write to files (it's dry-run so should not). The output earlier shows it just prints. Fine.\n\nAlso, should I check `doing --dry-run` and `human-loop --dry-run` too? The task plan KR4 mentions all three, but the TEST METHOD (task11_testgen_prompt.md) only specifies `./bin/rick plan --dry-run`. I'll follow the test method exactly: only `plan --dry-run`.\n\nLet me now also double check: does `./bin/rick plan --dry-run` require any config? It ran fine. It might fail if run from a different cwd or without config. I'll run with cwd=project_root.\n\nLet me also verify the `human-loop` and other subcommands exist, but not needed.\n\nNow, let 