{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"I now understand the migration target. The migration will replace \"Main Agent\"/\"Sub Agent\" (doing_loop), \"父 Agent\"/\"子 Agent\" (learning_loop), \"SPAWN Sub Agent\"/\"SPAWN Step\" with explicit `workflowScript` + `runs.run(agent:'worker')` syntax. So these role labels WILL be replaced. Good — my regex captures exactly what will be rewritten.\n\nNow I'm confident in the design. Let me write the test script.\n\nLet me also double-check the real agent name pattern. The test method uses: `grep -rcE \"agent:'worker'|agent:'reviewer'|agent:'think'|agent:'research'|agent:'exporter'\" internal/prompt/templates/ | grep -v ':0' | wc -l`. Note this counts FILES (lines from grep -c are per-file counts; `grep -v ':0'` filters zero; `wc -l` counts files). Expected ≥1.\n\nI'll replicate: count files (recursively under templates/) where at least one match of AGENT_NAME_PATTERN occurs. Expected ≥1.\n\nBut careful: `agent:'worker'` uses single quotes. In the templates, the migration will write `agent:'worker'`. My regex should match literally `agent:'worker'` etc. I'll use a compiled pattern with those literal strings.\n\nActually, note: the workflowScript examples might use `agent:'worker'` (single quotes) per the pi official example. Let me match exactly as the test method specifies: `agent:'worker'|agent:'reviewer'|agent:'think'|agent:'research'|agent:'exporter'`.\n\nFor the pi syntax pattern: `workflowScript|runs\\.run|runs\\.all` — I'll use `workflowScript|runs\\.run|runs\\.all`. Note \"runs.run\" — in the test method it's `runs\\.run`. I'll match `runs\\.run` and `runs\\.all`.\n\nNow let me also decide: for counting files with pi syntax, should I match `workflowScript` case-sensitively? Yes, pi syntax is `workflowScript` (camelCase). The test method regex is case-sensitive. I'll be case-sensitive for `workflowScript`, `runs.run`, `runs.all`, and agent names (they're lowercase). For SENSE semantics, Chinese terms + `judgment.md` (case-sensitive). For trigger words, case-insensitive (to catch \"Main Agent\" and \"main agent\").\n\nLet me now write the script.\n\nStructure:\n1. Shebang + docstring explaining the regex and workflow.\n2. imports.\n3. Constants (paths, patterns).\n4. Helper functions:\n   - `iter_md_files(templates_dir)` → yields md file paths recursively.\n   - `count_matches(pattern, files)` → total occurrences via finditer.\n   - `count_files_with_match(pattern, files)` → number of files with ≥1 match.\n   - `run_cmd(cmd_list, cwd, timeout)` → returns (returncode, stdout, stderr) via subprocess.\n   - `read_baseline(path)` → returns int or None.\n5. `main()`:\n   - Handle `--dump-baseline` flag.\n   - Collect errors.\n   - Normal path test.\n   - Boundary test.\n   - Exception test.\n   - Output JSON.\n\nLet me be careful with the JSON output: exactly one line to stdout. All debug to stderr.\n\nFor subprocess commands:\n- `go build ./...` — use `['go', 'build', './...']`, cwd=project_root. But the test method says `go build` (without ./...). `go build` alone in a module builds the package in current dir. `go build ./...` builds all. The test method literally says `go build`. I'll use `go build ./...` to be safe? Actually the test method says \"go build\" and \"go test ./internal/prompt/... -v\". Hmm. To match exactly, I could run `go build` (which builds current package) and `go build ./...`. But `go build` in repo root — the root has no .go files (only go.mod, and a test_parser.go at root!). Actually there's `test_parser.go` at root. `go build` in root would build the root package (which has test_parser.go with `package main`?). Let me check. Actually, simpler: run `go build ./...` which is the standard \"build everything\" and matches \"go build\" intent. But to be faithful, I'll run `go build ./...` — it's a superset and more thorough. Actually, the test method's \"go build\" is shorthand. I'll use `go build ./...`.\n\nWait, let me reconsider: `go build` with no args in a module root builds the main package(s) in the current directory. The root has `test_parser.go`. Let me check what package it is."}}
